@prefix chx: <http://example.org/sen0414/ch09#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/sen0414/ch09/diagrams> a owl:Ontology ; rdfs:label "SEN0414 ch09 narrative diagrams - ABox"@en ; owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ; dcterms:identifier "sen0414_ch09_diagrams_abox_v1_0_0" ;
    rdfs:comment "The chapter's authored diagrams as cme:NarrativeDiagram individuals: generated by sen0414_page_data_v2_6_0.py from sen0414_ch09_diagrams_v1_0_0.py, typed by the CME narrative-to-diagram mapping (a module of cme_standards_adoption_v1_3_0.ttl, CME 0.16.0)."@en ; owl:imports <http://example.org/cme/narrative-diagram> .

chx:Diagram_ChoosingACharacterRule a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Choosing the rule for one position in a pattern"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the character sets section: a character class in square brackets matches one character from a set, a caret negates the class, a shorthand such as digit or word or whitespace stands for a class, and the dot matches almost any character" ; cme:elementCount 14 ;
    cme:accompanies "CharacterClass" ; cme:accompanies "NegativeClass" ; cme:accompanies "ShorthandClasses" ; cme:accompanies "DotCharacter" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what a programmer decides in order when one position of a pattern is being written - is almost any character allowed, is a shorthand such as digit enough, should the set be negated, or does the position need an exact set of characters - with a different choice made on each branch: a workflow with choices on the way"@en .
chx:Diagram_ChoosingARepetition a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Choosing how many times a qualifier repeats"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the repetition section: a question mark makes an item optional, a star matches zero or more, a plus matches one or more, curly brackets give an exact count or a range, and a trailing question mark on any quantifier makes it lazy instead of greedy" ; cme:elementCount 19 ;
    cme:accompanies "OptionalQuantifier" ; cme:accompanies "StarQuantifier" ; cme:accompanies "PlusQuantifier" ; cme:accompanies "BraceQuantifier" ; cme:accompanies "GreedyAndLazy" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what a programmer decides in order when a quantifier is being chosen - is the item optional, is a range of counts needed, is the match meant to be the smallest one possible rather than the largest - with a different quantifier chosen on each branch: a workflow with choices on the way"@en .
chx:Diagram_PatternToReplacedText a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "A pattern's life from a raw string to a replaced string"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:LifeCycle ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:StateDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the pattern objects and replacing sections: a pattern is written as a raw string, compile turns it into a Pattern object, search returns a Match object or none, and sub returns a new string with every match replaced" ; cme:elementCount 9 ;
    cme:accompanies "RawStrings" ; cme:accompanies "CompileAndSearch" ; cme:accompanies "MatchObjectAccess" ; cme:accompanies "SubMethod" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe the states one piece of text passes through on its way from a pattern string to a replaced string - written raw, compiled, matched, replaced - and the operation that moves it from one state to the next: a life cycle"@en .
chx:Diagram_FindallShape a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "How findall decides the shape of what it returns"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Interaction ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:SequenceDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the finding and grouping sections: findall searches the text for every non-overlapping match, and the number of groups the pattern has decides whether a plain list of strings or a list of tuples of group text is returned" ; cme:elementCount 7 ;
    cme:accompanies "FindallMethod" ; cme:accompanies "FindallNoOverlap" ; cme:accompanies "GroupingParentheses" ; rdfs:comment "the passage is an exchange between parties in an order that matters - the pattern is searched against the text, every non-overlapping match is found, and the pattern's own groups decide the shape of the result handed back: an interaction"@en .
chx:Diagram_ContactExtractorUses a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What Project 3's contact extractor lets a user do"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:SetOfUses ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:UseCaseDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the contact extractor section: the user copies text onto the clipboard, the program searches it with a phone pattern and an email pattern, reads the groups of each match, joins every match found, and copies the result back" ; cme:elementCount 9 ;
    cme:accompanies "ClipboardRoundTrip" ; cme:accompanies "PhoneRegex" ; cme:accompanies "EmailRegex" ; cme:accompanies "ExtensionGroupIndex" ; rdfs:comment "the passage lists what the user and the program each do with text on the clipboard - copy text in, search with two patterns, read the groups, join the matches, copy text out - a set of uses by actors, not a sequence"@en .
chx:Diagram_PatternSyntaxAtAGlance a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Pattern syntax at a glance: four questions, one rule each"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:TopicAndParts ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:MindMap ; cme:readFrom "chapter 9, the opening of the pattern syntax branch and its four subsections: a pattern decides what to match, how many times, where, and what to keep, and the subsections group the syntax by grouping, by character sets, by repetition and by anchors" ; cme:elementCount 17 ;
    cme:accompanies "PatternSyntax" ; cme:accompanies "Grouping" ; cme:accompanies "CharacterSets" ; cme:accompanies "Repetition" ; cme:accompanies "Anchors" ; rdfs:comment "the section classifies one topic, pattern syntax, by the question each group of notation answers, and lists examples under each - a topic and its parts, laid out to remember, with the one question that holds for every part stated once at the root"@en .
